-- title:
--   p-adic eigencharacter on the rational Hecke algebra of J₁(M)
-- statement:
--   Let $M\ge 1$, let $\varepsilon$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $M$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$, and let $h$ be a weight-$2$ cusp form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ which is an eigenform with nebentypus $\varepsilon$ in the coefficient sense: its $q$-expansion satisfies $a_1(h)=1$, for every prime $q\nmid M$ and every $n$ one has $a_{qn}(h)+\varepsilon(q)\,q\,[q\mid n]\,a_{n/q}(h)=a_q(h)a_n(h)$, for every prime $\ell\mid M$ one has $a_{\ell n}(h)=a_\ell(h)a_n(h)$, and $h(\gamma\tau)=\varepsilon(\gamma_{11})(\gamma_{10}\tau+\gamma_{11})^2h(\tau)$ for all $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M)$. Let $p$ be a prime. Assume [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58), i.e. the predicate `HeckeInputsOneAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for every prime $\ell$ together with, for each $d$ coprime to $M$, the existence of a diamond automorphism of the $q$-expansion function field $x_1$ of level $M$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ in the sense of `IsDiamondAut` and of an automorphism of the base-changed function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which is a base change of it; assume also [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), that the generators `heckeDiamondGenBar M` commute pairwise, so that `heckeModuleOneBar M` makes the Hecke–diamond ring act on $J_1(M)=\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed function field. Let $S\subseteq\mathbb{N}$, let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field that is a $\mathbb{Q}_p$-algebra, let $R$ be a commutative ring with an injective ring homomorphism $\iota\colon R\to\mathbb{C}$ and a ring homomorphism $\psi\colon R\to\Omega$, and let $b,e\colon\mathbb{N}\to R$ satisfy $\iota(b_\ell)=a_\ell(h)$ and $\iota(e_\ell)=\varepsilon(\ell\bmod M)$ for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$. Then there is a ring homomorphism $\Lambda$ from the $\mathbb{Q}_p$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{Q}_p}(\mathbb{Q}_p\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}T_pJ_1(M))$ generated by the image of the Hecke–diamond ring to $\Omega$ such that $\Lambda$ restricts to the structure map on $\mathbb{Q}_p$, and such that for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$ one has $\Lambda(T_\ell)\,\psi(e_\ell)=\psi(b_\ell)$ and $\Lambda(\langle\ell\rangle)\,\psi(e_\ell)=1$.
--
--   This transfers the eigen-packet $(a_\ell(h),\varepsilon(\ell))_{\ell}$ of a normalised eigenform of weight $2$ and nebentypus $\varepsilon$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ into a character, with values in an algebraically closed $\mathbb{Q}_p$-algebra, of the $\mathbb{Q}_p$-algebra generated by the Hecke operators and diamond operators acting on the rational Tate module of $J_1(M)$; the normalisation is the adjoint one, the relations being stated multiplicatively as $\Lambda(T_\ell)\varepsilon(\ell)=a_\ell$ and $\Lambda(\langle\ell\rangle)\varepsilon(\ell)=1$ so that no inverses occur. It is the input to the construction of the $p$-adic Galois representation attached to $h$ inside the Tate module, whose characteristic polynomials of Frobenius and ramification properties are recorded in the statements that cite it.
